            <ArticleTitle>Intravesical foreign body: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Foreign bodies of the urinary bladder may occur by self insertion or migration from the neighbouring organs. All the foreign bodies when left for long act as a nidus for calculus formation. The patient usually presents with dysuria, intermittent urinary tract infection or suprapubic pain. Here we report two cases of vesical foreign body which was removed by the cystoscopy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
